{"conjecture": "Long-running systems degrade not through individual errors but through the silent accumulation of *implicit invariants*—unstated assumptions about behavior that no single change violates, but that collectively shrink the space of future valid changes. Each locally correct modification preserves all *known* invariants while unknowingly tightening *unknown* ones, so the system's true constraint surface grows monotonically even as its documented interface stays fixed. This predicts a measurable quantity—*change impedance*—which rises as a function of the ratio of undocumented behavioral dependencies to explicit interface contracts, and which can be reversed only by making previously implicit invariants explicit through refactoring, not by improving the quality of individual changes."}
